    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 181921\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1121 AM PST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* A low pressure system will bring snow to the mountains of Inyo \n  County and southern Nevada beginning today and continuing \n  through Friday morning. \n\n* This low pressure system will also bring gusty southerly-to-\n  southwesterly wind to the region over the next few days.\n\n* There will be a brief reprieve from unsettled weather over the\n  weekend before the next system impacts the area early next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Wednesday.\n\nCurrent satellite imagery shows a closed upper-low pushing down the \nPacific Northwest coast. This system will continue to dig southeast \nover the next 24 to 36 hours, bringing unsettled weather to \nsoutheastern California, southern Nevada, and northwestern Arizona \nthrough Friday. Latest radar also shows a wide area of precipitation \npushing into central California with areas of moderate to heavy \nrain, along with snow for the higher elevations. As the day goes on, \nprecipitation will increase over the southern Sierra, bringing \nwidespread snow to the eastern Sierra this afternoon through \nThursday morning. The bulk of the heavier snow is forecast to fall \nin the Sierra, where 3 to 4 feet is possible for the higher \nelevations and 2 to 3 feet for areas like Aspendell (8,400 feet). \nEven elevations between 6,000 and 8,000 feet could see around a foot \nof snow. A Winter Storm Warning remains in effect for the Eastern \nSierra through Friday morning and looks to be on track. Rain is \nexpected in the Owens Valley with totals generally between 0.50\" and \n1.00\" by Thursday evening. Precipitation amounts will drop off \nfurther east, though the White Mountains could still see a few \ninches of snow, where a Winter Weather Advisory is in effect through \nFriday morning.\n\nThe main band of precipitation is projected to push east early \nThursday morning, continue through the day, and exit the area \nThursday night. Valley rainfall amounts will remain generally light \noutside of Inyo County, with most areas seeing less than a tenth of \nan inch. In the Spring Mountains, a Winter Weather Advisory is in \neffect from 4:00 AM PST Thursday through early Friday morning. For \nelevations between 7,000 and 9,500 feet, storm-total snow \naccumulations of 3 to 9 inches are forecast. Looking specifically at \nthe Mount Charleston and Lee Canyon areas, 4 to 8 inches looks to be \nthe most likely scenario, with a 10% chance of seeing 10 inches or \ngreater.\n\nIn addition to the mountain snow and valley rain, this system will \nbring gusty south to southwest winds today and Thursday. The \nstrongest winds today will remain confined to the northwest \nCWAincluding Inyo, Esmeralda, and central and southern Nye \ncountieswhere sustained winds of 20 to 30 mph and gusts to 45 mph \nare expected. Wind Advisories are in effect for these areas from \n4:00 PM this afternoon through Thursday morning. As the system \npushes east, gusty winds will develop Thursday afternoon through \nThursday evening across San Bernardino, southern Clark, and Mohave \ncounties, with gusts between 40 and 45 mph prompting additional Wind \nAdvisories. Expect difficult driving conditions for high-profile \nvehicles, and use extra caution as blowing dust near dry lakebeds \nmay reduce visibility.\n\nConditions will improve throughout the day Friday into Saturday as \nthe trough exits the region. While temperatures will remain cooler \nthan normal through the weekend, weak, transient ridging will \nprovide a brief reprieve from the precipitation and wind. This quiet \nstretch will be short-lived, however, as another system looks to \nimpact the region early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...\nEasterly winds under 10 kts into the early afternoon then expect \nwinds to veer toward the ESE by 21z with sporadic gusts to 15 kt \nthrough sunset. Winds becoming light and variable winds after 02z, \nfavoring a NW direction after 08z and remaining under 6 kt. Mid and \nhigh clouds will increase beyond 00z Thursday with ceilings at or \nbelow 9000ft possible after 18z Thursday, lowering further during \nthe afternoon with rain expected by 21z. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Mid/high clouds will \nincrease from west to east during the afternoon. Strongest winds \nthrough the period at KBIH with sustained winds 15-20 kt and gusts \nexceeding 35 kt at times through Thursday afternoon. CIGs will lower \nstarting this evening at KBIH with rain moving in and CIGs will \ncontinue fall overnight lowering below BKN020 by Thursday morning. \nMost other terminals can expect winds at 10 kts or less with \nisolated gusts to 15 kt during the afternoon.  \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow\nAVIATION...Czyzyk\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
